          "heading": "Rebuilding a World Economy: Bretton Woods",
          "para": "Out of the wreckage of the Depression, economists and politicians drew two lessons: mass consumption could not be sustained without high and stable incomes, and stable incomes required steady employment, which governments had to guarantee by managing their economies. The task of rebuilding was given to a new framework. In July 1944 the United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference met at Bretton Woods in New Hampshire, USA, and its delegates created the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank (the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development) to stabilise currencies and finance post-war reconstruction; both institutions began operations in 1947, though voting power remained with the Western industrial countries. The Bretton Woods system rested on fixed exchange rates: member currencies were pegged to the US dollar, and the dollar itself - convertible into gold at a fixed rate - became the anchor and main reserve currency of world trade. The system worked: from the 1950s to the 1960s the West enjoyed a 'golden age' of low unemployment, soaring production and welfare states, with world trade growing at over 8 per cent a year.",
