          "para": "Even before factories appeared, Europe had a world of industry. In the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, merchants from the towns of Europe moved out into the countryside and supplied money and raw materials to peasants and artisans, persuading them to produce for an international market. This phase is called proto-industrialisation, because 'proto' means a coming-before: it was large-scale production for the world market that existed before machine factories. Merchants went to the countryside for good reason. Towns were dominated by guilds - associations of craftsmen that controlled who could trade, what prices were charged and how apprentices were trained - and they stood in the way of new merchants seeking cheap, unregulated production. Poor peasants and artisans, whose incomes from land were shrinking, were eager to earn by working from their cottages. The whole family joined in: men, women and children carded, spun and wove. Merchants collected the finished cloth and took it to finishing centres in London, where it was dyed, finished and shipped to international markets. Thus thousands of rural families were tied to a world market long before the first factory chimney rose.",
