          "para": "Cotton was the symbol of the new industrial age, and the earliest factories in England had come up by the 1730s, though their number multiplied only in the late eighteenth century. The decisive step was taken by Richard Arkwright, who created the cotton mill at Cromford in Derbyshire in 1771. Arkwright's insight was to gather costly machines and hundreds of workers under one roof and drive the machines by water power, so that production no longer depended on scattered families working at their own pace in cottages. Inside the mill a new world of discipline appeared: workers had to arrive at fixed hours, keep to their machines, follow supervisors and work at the rhythm the mill set, rather than the rhythm of family and season. Supervision and discipline raised output, standardised quality and cut costs, which cottage production could not match. The factory, therefore, became the new organisation of production - and soon an intimate part of the English landscape.",
