          "heading": "Peculiarities of Industrial Growth in India",
          "para": "Industrial growth in India followed a peculiar pattern of its own. Till the First World War it was slow: the early mills exported coarse cotton yarn to China rather than weaving cloth for India, since Manchester cloth dominated the home market. European managing agencies, with their capital and contacts, held the capital-intensive sectors - jute mills, tea and coffee plantations and mines - while Indian merchants and financiers clustered in smaller enterprises. The war changed the balance: British imports were cut off, Indian mills were called upon to supply the army and the home market with cloth and jute goods, and factory production expanded rapidly; by the war's end Indian industry had established itself, though Manchester fought hard to recover its share afterwards. Handloom weaving did not die either: weavers used cheap mill-spun yarn and specialised in fine, intricate varieties - richly patterned saris and elaborate borders - for better-off customers, so machine and hand technology coexisted. Cheap labour, varied demand and the taste for distinctive designs kept hand production alive alongside the mill.",
