          "para": "To sell goods, manufacturers had to create demand, and in colonial India advertising did this work in striking ways. When Manchester industrialists began selling cloth in India, they placed paper labels on the cloth bundles: the label served as a guarantee of quality, a way of marking the product in a market where buyers could not easily judge cloth. The words 'MADE IN MANCHESTER' were written in bold on round labels, and the labels carried images of Indian gods and goddesses, so that divine approval seemed to bless the imported cloth. Figures of emperors and nawabs were used in advertisements to win respect and suggest that the products carried royal prestige. Calendars became clever free gifts: printed with the images of gods, famous personalities and the products themselves, they hung in shops and homes and were seen every day of the year, quietly fixing the brand in memory. Indian mill owners answered with the same weapons, printing gods and respected figures on their own labels to claim quality and legitimacy, while newspapers and street hoardings carried the message further. Advertising thus helped build the very market it served.",
